WEBVTT

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.461
We’re Optimistic Mutant Coders. This is Little Forge.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:05.935
Here’s the pinned Flask release.

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:11.106
Choose from eight palettes, or follow your VS Code theme.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:19.544
The extension streams turns from the local TrueForge harness. Editor actions are exposed through MCP.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.630
Use the picker to switch between models routed through TrueForge.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:31.951
For Task A, I select the entire debug flag function and ask for two more false values.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.229
The edit is scoped to this selection.

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:44.715
I review the docstring and behavior changes as separate hunks, then apply both.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:53.133
The diff adds off and n to the false values.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.299
Source Control shows one modified file.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:04.377
With the exact accepted edit, setting Flask Debug to off returns false.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:12.152
For Task B, I request a config command in Build mode, without naming any files.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:27.103
The agent retrieves relevant code within a twelve thousand token budget, then reads the implementation, tests, and docs.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.382
I review seven hunks across four files.

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.761
Each file has its own review controls.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:53.933
After review, I apply the proposal. The receipt confirms four files were changed.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:02.200
All sixty CLI tests pass in the VS Code terminal.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.157
The graph shows B’s four files, plus the earlier inline edit.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.933
Flask help lists config. The demo app’s public label stays visible, and all four sensitive values are masked.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:29.334
This is the same TrueForge session. Here are its editor reads, searches, and proposal calls.

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:43.107
I select an unreachable model endpoint while TrueForge stays connected. Then I send the same inline request.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:55.403
The model request retries twice. I can still navigate the editor, and the extension surfaces a connection error.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:08.361
The error offers a retry and a link to inspect the session.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.440
Source Control shows an empty Changes pane.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:19.927
The follow up check matches all two hundred thirty five tracked files to the baseline.

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:26.993
The Tools drawer brings together workspace signals, project rules, and the session link.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.917
The Task Contract defines what the agent can change and what needs approval.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.285
Repro to Test turns a bug report into a red green regression workflow.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:46.234
Here’s the editor MCP connector registered in the local TrueForge harness.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.933
That’s Little Forge. Thanks for watching.
